.\" ============================================================================
.\" compose-upgrade.1 - manual page
.\" ============================================================================
.TH COMPOSE-UPGRADE 1 "2025-12-06" "compose-upgrade 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
compose-upgrade \- safely upgrade one or more Docker Compose projects

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compose-upgrade
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " [PROJECT_DIR ...]"

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compose-upgrade
is a helper utility for upgrading one or more Docker Compose projects in a
consistent, auditable way. It is intended for long-lived services that are
defined by a Compose file and managed with
.BR "docker compose"
or
.BR docker-compose .
The tool performs configuration validation, pulls updated images, and recreates
containers using the Compose project configuration.

If one or more
.I PROJECT_DIR
arguments are specified, each directory is treated as the root of a Docker
Compose project. If no project directory is given, the current directory
.RB ( . )
is used. A project directory is considered valid if it contains at least one of
the following files:
.IR docker-compose.yml ,
.IR docker-compose.yaml ,
.IR compose.yml ,
or
.IR compose.yaml .

This command is intentionally focused on Compose-managed workloads and does not
attempt to upgrade arbitrary containers created with ad-hoc
.B docker run
commands. For such containers, use a separate utility or migrate them into
Compose projects before using
.BR compose-upgrade .

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Enable dry-run mode. When this option is used,
.B compose-upgrade
prints the actions it would perform but does not execute any Docker or Docker
Compose commands. This is useful for validating which projects would be
touched and in which order.

.TP
.B \-\-prune\-images
After all projects have been processed and if no project failed, run
.BR "docker image prune \-f"
to remove dangling images. This can reclaim disk space from unused layers after
upgrading stacks that track
.B :latest
or other moving tags. If any project fails, the prune step is skipped.

.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file " FILE"
Append log output to
.IR FILE
in addition to standard error. The file is created if it does not exist. This
is useful when
.B compose-upgrade
is used from cron, systemd timers, or other unattended automation that
requires persistent logs.

.TP
.B \-\-strict
Enable strict mode. When this option is set,
.B compose-upgrade
exits immediately with a non-zero status as soon as any project fails to
upgrade. Without strict mode, the command continues processing remaining
projects and reports a summary at the end, returning a non-zero status if any
project failed.

.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color codes in log output. This is useful when logs are being
captured by tooling that does not handle color codes correctly.

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a brief usage summary and exit.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COMPOSE_UPGRADE_CMD
If set, overrides the Docker Compose command used by
.BR compose-upgrade .
The value is split on whitespace and executed as a command.
This can be used to force a particular binary or wrapper, for example:
.RS
.nf
COMPOSE_UPGRADE_CMD="docker compose"
COMPOSE_UPGRADE_CMD="/usr/local/bin/docker-compose"
.fi
.RE

.SH OPERATION
For each project directory,
.B compose-upgrade
performs the following steps:

.IP 1. 4
Verify that the directory exists and contains at least one recognized Compose
file. If no Compose file is found, the project is reported as failed.

.IP 2. 4
Run
.BR "docker compose config"
or
.BR "docker-compose config"
to validate the Compose configuration. In dry-run mode, this is logged but not
executed.

.IP 3. 4
Run
.BR "docker compose pull"
or
.BR "docker-compose pull"
to pull updated images referenced by the project.

.IP 4. 4
Run
.BR "docker compose up \-d \-\-remove\-orphans"
or the equivalent
.BR docker-compose
command to recreate containers in detached mode and remove orphaned containers
no longer defined in the Compose configuration.

If
.BR \-\-prune\-images
is specified, and all projects complete successfully, a final
.BR "docker image prune \-f"
is performed to remove dangling images. In dry-run mode, all of these
operations are logged but not executed.

.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of
.B compose-upgrade
is intended to be useful for scripting and automation.

.TP
.B 0
All requested projects were processed successfully and, if requested, image
pruning completed without error.

.TP
.B 1
One or more errors occurred. This can include missing prerequisites (for
example, Docker or Docker Compose not installed, or the Docker daemon not
available), invalid project directories, missing Compose files, or failures in
Compose operations. When
.BR \-\-strict
is used, this status is returned immediately upon the first project failure.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Upgrade the Compose project in the current directory:
.RS
.nf
compose-upgrade
.fi
.RE

.TP
Upgrade two specific projects under
.BR /opt :
.RS
.nf
compose-upgrade /opt/psa /opt/rmm
.fi
.RE

.TP
Show what would be done, without executing any Docker commands:
.RS
.nf
compose-upgrade --dry-run /opt/psa /opt/rmm
.fi
.RE

.TP
Upgrade all specified projects, prune dangling images if successful, and log to a file:
.RS
.nf
compose-upgrade --prune-images \\
                --log-file /var/log/compose-upgrade.log \\
                /opt/psa /opt/rmm
.fi
.RE

.TP
Use strict mode so that a failure in one project stops processing immediately:
.RS
.nf
compose-upgrade --strict /opt/psa /opt/rmm /opt/cloud
.fi
.RE

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/compose-upgrade
Typical installation path for the
.B compose-upgrade
executable script.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR docker (1),
.BR docker-compose (1)

